    "snippet": "---\ntitle: The Indian Question in Afrocentric Politics: A Continent's Reckoning with the Ugandan Expulsion Available\nauthor: Shobana Shankar\ndate: 2024-09-01\n---\nAbstract\n\nFor African-Indian relationships, the idealism of Afro-Asian solidarity did not often match realities on the ground, which were volatile and rapidly changing from the 1940s. This essay explores how Idi Amin's expulsion of Asians was embedded in a racial calculus that had a history beyond Uganda in 1972, in the context of ongoing struggles for African independence, Pan-Africanism, the Cold War, and religious nationalism in the Middle East. The Indian question in Africa was a political and sociocultural one\u2014emblematic of division and partition based on ethno-religious and cultural difference and hierarchy\u2014and not simply an economic one for Africans. The Ugandan expulsion was complex and not a single event but one made in multiple registers, as wider continental African perspectives reveal.\n\nIn the early twentieth century, Afro-Asian solidarity signified a hopeful union, evoking a range of political aspirations and cultural projects. Although many conventional stories begin with the Afro-Asian conference in 1955 in Bandung, Indonesia, other Afro-Asian visions prefigured this. From the early 1900s, African American thinker W. E. B. Du Bois linked Africa, the African diaspora, and Asia in the struggle against the global color line and as an economic imperative against capitalism (Mullen and Watson 2005). In the World War II era, actor and activist Paul Robeson performed songs in Chinese in Harlem, in a show of Black American support for communism. In a different vein of Afro-Asian possibility, Ethiopian intellectuals in the 1920s looked to Japan as a model for their modernization (Marzagora 2023). Optimism also grew in the religious arena in the 1920s, when pan-Islamism was understood by some Africans, Indians, and Japanese, among others, as a viable movement to achieve racial and social equality in a world of inequality and violence (Aydin 2016; Shankar 2020, 2021; Koyagi 2013). Well before and beyond Bandung, Afro-Asian alliances promised alternatives to a world order made by Euro-American domination.\n\nBut for African-Indian relationships, idealism did not often match realities on the ground, which were messy and changing. In African colonies where Indians had"
