an archaic usage: to unite, or to join together materially (Allen, 1990, p. 43; Merriam-Webster, 2014, p. 50; Waite et al., 2008, p. 31). That \"archaic\" usage persists, however, and appropriately so.\n\nThe tension among these meanings persists in political science and law. In both disciplines, annexation can mean both lawful incorporation and unlawful expansion through seizure, conquest, and occupation. Any state that has carried out a seizure, conquest, or occupation may legally ratify its annexation domestically, but whether that annexation will be recognized"
