}"}, {"type": "output_text", "tool_name": null, "arguments": null, "output": "Explanation: The activist in question is Syngman Rhee, a Korean independence activist. Korea was the colonized country in this chain: Japan formally annexed Korea in 1910 after it had become a Japanese protectorate in 1905 [57336][24150]. In 1905, a Korean party led by Syngman Rhee (with Pyeng Yoon) traveled to the Portsmouth Conference to invoke the good offices promised to Korea under the Joseon–United States Treaty of 1882, and the delegation met with U.S. President Theodore Roosevelt to present a memorial documenting Japan's mistreatment of Korea [11997]. The Portsmouth Conference was held in the United States, and the whole purpose of the mission was to appeal to the U.S. government on behalf of Korean independence, which places this mission squarely within the 1900–1950 window (1905) [11997]. Therefore, the country to which the activist was sent to promote his country's independence was the United States [11997].
